# Parsers and combinators

A grammar written with this library is a set of Avin values joined by operators that read like the
grammar itself:

```avin
val number = token(integer)
val sum = chainLeft(number, symbol("+") ^^^ ((a: long, b: long) -> a + b))
sum.parse("1 + 2 + 3")          // Ok(6)
```

`p ~ q` is `p` then `q`, `p | q` is `p` or else `q`, and `p ^^ f` turns what `p` read into a value.
This file defines the parser itself and every way of joining parsers. The parsers that read text
directly, literals, names, numbers and strings, are in `leaves.lavin`; the input they read is in
`input.lavin`.

    module dev.avinlang.parsing

## What a parser is

A parser is a function from an `Input` to an `Option`. It answers `Some(value)` having moved the
input's offset past what it read, or `None` having recorded at the input what it expected. That is
the whole contract, and anything that keeps it is a parser: the library's own combinators are
written exactly as a user's custom parser would be.

`Parser` is a class rather than a bare function type so that the combinators can be its methods,
and so that `p | q` can be written as an operator. It is covariant: a `Parser[Dog]` is a
`Parser[Animal]`.

    // A parser of a `T`. `run` reads from the input's offset; a custom parser is made by giving one.
    class Parser[+T](val run: Input -> Option[T])

## Sequence

The combinators are methods of an `impl` of `Parser[T]`. Methods named by symbols bind by their
first character, so every name with a `~` in it binds tightest, then `^^` and `^^^`, then `|`. A
rule like `a ~ b ^^ f | c` therefore needs no parentheses: it is `((a ~ b) ^^ f) | c`.

Every parser a combinator is given, other than the one it is a method of, is taken **by name**:
`p ~ q` does not read `q` when it is called, but the first time the parser it makes runs, and keeps
it. That is what lets a grammar refer to itself. Module-level rules are `lazy val`s, each made the
first time it is read, and making `expr` reads `atom`, which mentions `expr` again before it is
complete. Because `atom` only mentions it, as an operand that is read later, the cycle is never
followed while the rules are being made:

```avin
lazy val expr: Parser[long] = chainLeft(term, addOp)
lazy val atom: Parser[long] = token(integer) | symbol("(") ~> expr <~ symbol(")")
```

The one place a rule is read at once is the receiver of a method: in `p | q`, `p` is made when the
choice is. A rule that begins with itself, `expr ~ symbol("+") ~ term`, loops for ever when run in
any case, so a grammar never needs to.

`p ~ q` runs `p`, then `q` where `p` stopped, and pairs their values. If either fails the pair
fails, and the failing parser has already recorded what it expected.

    impl[T] Parser[T]
        // This parser, then `next`: both values.
        ~[U](next: -> Parser[U]) -> Parser[(T, U)]
            val q = later(next)
            Parser(input ->
                run(input) match
                    Some(a) -> q.run(input) match
                        Some(b) -> Some((a, b))
                        None -> None
                    None -> None)

Much of a grammar is punctuation whose value nobody wants: the brackets around a list, the colon
after a key. The arrow points at the side that is kept: `p ~> q` keeps `q`'s value and `p <~ q`
keeps `p`'s, so `symbol("[") ~> items <~ symbol("]")` is the items alone.

        // This parser, then `next`, whose value is kept.
        ~>[U](next: -> Parser[U]) -> Parser[U]
            val q = later(next)
            Parser(input ->
                run(input) match
                    Some(_) -> q.run(input)
                    None -> None)

        // This parser, then `next`, whose value is dropped.
        <~[U](next: -> Parser[U]) -> Parser[T]
            val q = later(next)
            Parser(input ->
                run(input) match
                    Some(a) -> q.run(input) match
                        Some(_) -> Some(a)
                        None -> None
                    None -> None)

`p ~! q` is `p ~ q` with a **commit point** between them. Once `p` has succeeded the grammar knows
which rule it is in, so a failure of `q` sets the input's `committed` flag, and every alternative
above it gives up instead of trying another branch from the start. After `lit("let") ~! ident`, a
missing name is reported as the name `let` wanted, rather than as whatever the next kind of
statement would have begun with.

        // This parser, then `next`, committed: once this parser has succeeded, a failure of `next` fails
        // the whole parse instead of letting an alternative be tried.
        ~![U](next: -> Parser[U]) -> Parser[(T, U)]
            val q = later(next)
            Parser(input ->
                run(input) match
                    Some(a) -> q.run(input) match
                        Some(b) -> Some((a, b))
                        None ->
                            input.committed = true
                            None
                    None -> None)

## Choice

`p | q` tries `p`, and when it fails tries `q` **from where `p` started**, however far `p` had read.
Backtracking is a single assignment, since the offset is the only thing a parser moves. A
committed failure is not retried.

        // This parser, or `other` from the same place when this one fails before a commit point.
        |(other: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[T]
            val q = later(other)
            Parser(input ->
                val start = input.offset
                run(input) match
                    Some(a) -> Some(a)
                    None ->
                        if input.committed then return None
                        input.offset = start
                        q.run(input))

## Values

`p ^^ f` applies `f` to what `p` read; `map` is the same under a name. `p ^^^ v` replaces it with a
fixed value, which is how a keyword becomes a constant: `symbol("null") ^^^ JNull`.

        // The value given to `f`.
        ^^[U](f: T -> U) -> Parser[U] = map(f)

        map[U](f: T -> U) -> Parser[U] = Parser(input ->
            run(input) match
                Some(a) -> Some(f(a))
                None -> None)

        // `value` in place of what was read.
        ^^^[U](value: U) -> Parser[U] = Parser(input ->
            run(input) match
                Some(_) -> Some(value)
                None -> None)

`flatMap` lets what was read choose what is read next: a closing tag that must repeat the opening
one, a length followed by that many items.

        // The parser `f` makes of the value, run where this one stopped.
        flatMap[U](f: T -> Parser[U]) -> Parser[U] = Parser(input ->
            run(input) match
                Some(a) -> f(a).run(input)
                None -> None)

`filter` refuses a value the grammar can read but does not accept: a name that is a keyword, a
number out of range. The refusal is reported where the value began, since that is where the text
went wrong, and `p` is moved back there so an alternative can try the same text.

        // The value when `p` accepts it; otherwise a failure where this parser started, expecting `what`.
        filter(what: string, p: T -> bool) -> Parser[T] = Parser(input ->
            val start = input.offset
            run(input) match
                Some(a) if p(a) -> Some(a)
                Some(_) ->
                    input.offset = start
                    input.fail(what)
                None -> None)

## Errors

`label` and `commit` change what a failure says or does, not what is parsed. Both are explained
with the input in `input.lavin`: a label replaces a rule's expectations by its name when the rule
got no further than its first character, and a commit makes a failure final.

        // This parser, reported as expecting `what` when it fails without getting past its first
        // character, in place of the expectations of the parsers it is made of.
        label(what: string) -> Parser[T] = Parser(input ->
            val start = input.offset
            val mark = input.expectationsAt(start)
            val r = run(input)
            if r is None then input.relabel(start, mark, what)
            r)

        // This parser, whose failure fails the whole parse.
        commit() -> Parser[T] = Parser(input ->
            val r = run(input)
            if r is None then input.committed = true
            r)

## Optional parts

`opt` always succeeds: with `Some` of the value when the parser does, and with `None`, having read
nothing, when it does not.

        // `Some` of the value, or `None` without reading anything when this parser fails.
        opt() -> Parser[Option[T]] = Parser(input ->
            val start = input.offset
            run(input) match
                Some(a) -> Some(Some(a))
                None ->
                    if input.committed then return None
                    input.offset = start
                    Some(None))

## Positions

`positioned` pairs a value with where it began, for a syntax tree that reports errors of its own
later: a type checker's "not defined" at the name's line and column.

        // The value with the position it starts at.
        positioned() -> Parser[(T, Position)] = Parser(input ->
            val start = input.offset
            run(input) match
                Some(a) -> Some((a, input.position(start)))
                None -> None)

## Running a parser

`parse` is how a grammar is used: it reads the whole text or fails. A parser that succeeds but
stops short of the end has not parsed the text, so that is a failure expecting `end of input`,
recorded alongside whatever else could have continued there. For `1 + 2 )` the error says the
parser expected an operator or the end, and found `)`.

        // The value of the whole of `text`, or the error at the furthest place any alternative reached.
        parse(text: string) -> Result[T, ParseError]
            val input = Input(text)
            run(input) match
                Some(a) if input.atEnd() -> Ok(a)
                Some(_) ->
                    input.expect("end of input")
                    Err(input.error())
                None -> Err(input.error())

`parsePrefix` reads as much as the parser takes and says where it stopped, for text that goes on in
some other language: a number at the start of a line, a header before a body.

        // The value of the longest beginning of `text` this parser accepts, with the byte offset where it
        // stopped.
        parsePrefix(text: string) -> Result[(T, int), ParseError]
            val input = Input(text)
            run(input) match
                Some(a) -> Ok((a, input.offset))
                None -> Err(input.error())

## Repetition

A repetition is not something a parser does but something made of one, so it takes the parser it
repeats: `rep(statement)`, `repSep(value, symbol(","))`.

`rep` runs a parser as many times as it succeeds. One case needs care: a parser that can succeed
without reading anything, such as `takeWhile(isDigit)`, would succeed for ever at the same place.
So a run that reads nothing ends the repetition, and its value is not added. The loop itself is
`repeated`, below, shared by `rep` and `rep1`.

    // The values of `p` run as many times as it succeeds, none included. A run that reads nothing
    // ends the repetition without adding its value, so a parser that can succeed on nothing does not
    // loop.
    rep[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[Vector[T]]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input -> repeated(q, input, Buffer[T]()))

    // As `rep`, at least once.
    rep1[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[Vector[T]]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input ->
            q.run(input) match
                Some(a) ->
                    val items = Buffer[T]()
                    items.add(a)
                    repeated(q, input, items)
                None -> None)

`repSep` reads values with separators between them. A separator promises another value, so
`[1, 2,]` fails at the `]` rather than quietly accepting the trailing comma. The loop is
`separated`, below.

    // The values of `p` separated by `sep`, none included. A separator must be followed by a value,
    // and a separator and value that together read nothing end the repetition.
    repSep[T, S](p: -> Parser[T], sep: -> Parser[S]) -> Parser[Vector[T]]
        val q = later(p)
        val s = later(sep)
        Parser(input ->
            val start = input.offset
            q.run(input) match
                Some(a) -> separated(q, s, input, a)
                None ->
                    if input.committed then return None
                    input.offset = start
                    Some(Vector.empty[T]()))

    // As `repSep`, at least one value.
    rep1Sep[T, S](p: -> Parser[T], sep: -> Parser[S]) -> Parser[Vector[T]]
        val q = later(p)
        val s = later(sep)
        Parser(input ->
            q.run(input) match
                Some(a) -> separated(q, s, input, a)
                None -> None)

Both loops have the same shape: remember where the next run starts, run, and on failure go back
there and stop. They stop also when a run read nothing. A committed failure is not a stop but a
failure of the whole repetition.

    // `p` run as many times as it succeeds, added to `items`.
    private repeated[T](p: Parser[T], input: Input, items: Buffer[T]) -> Option[Vector[T]]
        loop
            val start = input.offset
            p.run(input) match
                Some(a) ->
                    if input.offset == start then break
                    items.add(a)
                None ->
                    if input.committed then return None
                    input.offset = start
                    break
        Some(items.toVector())

In `separated` the separator is optional but the value after it is not. A separator that fails ends
the list where it began; a value that fails after a separator fails the list, which is what makes
a trailing separator an error. `?` returns the `None` at once.

    // `first`, then the values of `p` after each `sep`.
    private separated[T, S](p: Parser[T], sep: Parser[S], input: Input, first: T) -> Option[Vector[T]]
        val items = Buffer[T]()
        items.add(first)
        loop
            val start = input.offset
            if sep.run(input) is None
                if input.committed then return None
                input.offset = start
                break
            val a = p.run(input)?
            if input.offset == start then break
            items.add(a)
        Some(items.toVector())

## The same, as functions

`opt`, `commit` and `positioned` are also functions of the parser, for grammars that read better
with them in front: `opt(sign)` beside `sign.opt()`.

    // `p.opt()`.
    opt[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[Option[T]] = later(p).opt()

    // `p.commit()`.
    commit[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[T] = later(p).commit()

    // `p.positioned()`.
    positioned[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[(T, Position)] = later(p).positioned()

## Operators and their grouping

An expression grammar is mostly levels of binary operators. Written as plain repetition each level
would yield a list for the user to fold; `chainLeft` folds as it reads. Its operator parser yields
the function that combines two values, so one parser can stand for several operators:
`op("+", add) | op("-", subtract)`.

`chainLeft` groups from the left, as subtraction and division do: `1 - 2 - 3` is `(1 - 2) - 3`. An
operator with no operand after it fails the chain, since the operator promised one.

    // One `p` or more separated by `op`, combined from the left by the functions `op` yields:
    // `1 - 2 - 3` is `(1 - 2) - 3`.
    chainLeft[T](p: -> Parser[T], op: -> Parser[(T, T) -> T]) -> Parser[T]
        val q = later(p)
        val o = later(op)
        Parser(input ->
            var acc = q.run(input)?
            loop
                val start = input.offset
                val f = o.run(input) match
                    Some(f) -> f
                    None ->
                        if input.committed then return None
                        input.offset = start
                        break
                acc = f(acc, q.run(input)?)
            Some(acc))

`chainRight` groups from the right, as exponentiation does: `2 ^ 3 ^ 2` is `2 ^ (3 ^ 2)`. It can
fold only once it has everything, so it collects the values and operators as it reads and folds
them from the end. It does this in a loop rather than by recursion, so a long chain costs no stack.

    // One `p` or more separated by `op`, combined from the right: `2 ^ 3 ^ 2` is `2 ^ (3 ^ 2)`.
    chainRight[T](p: -> Parser[T], op: -> Parser[(T, T) -> T]) -> Parser[T]
        val q = later(p)
        val o = later(op)
        Parser(input ->
            val values = Buffer[T]()
            val ops = Buffer[(T, T) -> T]()
            values.add(q.run(input)?)
            loop
                val start = input.offset
                val f = o.run(input) match
                    Some(f) -> f
                    None ->
                        if input.committed then return None
                        input.offset = start
                        break
                ops.add(f)
                values.add(q.run(input)?)
            var acc = values(values.length() - 1)
            var i = ops.length() - 1
            while i >= 0
                acc = ops(i)(values(i), acc)
                i -= 1
            Some(acc))

## Parsers of nothing

`success` reads nothing and yields a value; `failure` reads nothing and fails. They are the two
ends of a choice: a default at the end of a list of alternatives, or a branch of `flatMap` that
refuses.

    // Succeeds with `value`, reading nothing.
    success[T](value: T) -> Parser[T] = Parser(input -> Some(value))

    // Fails, expecting `what`.
    failure[T](what: string) -> Parser[T] = Parser(input -> input.fail(what))

## Recursive grammars

Every combinator takes its operands by name, as described under Sequence, and `later` is how it
keeps one: a parser that evaluates `p` the first time it runs, long after every rule is complete,
and keeps what it found. A by-name parameter is evaluated again at each read, so without `later` a
rule would be made anew at every step of every parse.

    // The parser `p` names, found the first time it runs rather than where `later` is called.
    private later[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[T]
        var made: Option[Parser[T]] = None
        Parser(input ->
            val q = made match
                Some(q) -> q
                None ->
                    val q = p
                    made = Some(q)
                    q
            q.run(input))

Each level of nesting is a few calls deep, so how deep a text can nest is bounded by the stack of
the thread parsing it. An arithmetic grammar takes a few thousand levels of parentheses on the main
thread, and a parse run on a thread spawned with a larger stack goes as deep as that stack allows.

## Looking ahead

Two parsers look at the text without reading it. `notFollowedBy(p)` succeeds where `p` fails: `if`
followed by a letter is the start of a name like `iffy`, not the keyword. It restores the commit
flag too, since a commit inside a look ahead commits nothing outside it.

    // Succeeds without reading anything where `p` fails, and fails where `p` succeeds.
    notFollowedBy[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[unit]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input ->
            val start = input.offset
            val committed = input.committed
            val r = q.run(input)
            input.offset = start
            input.committed = committed
            if r is None then Some(()) else None)

`guard(p)` succeeds where `p` does, with its value, and leaves the offset where it was.

    // The value of `p`, without reading anything: a look ahead.
    guard[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[T]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input ->
            val start = input.offset
            val r = q.run(input)
            input.offset = start
            r)
